# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from nums import *
Cv = C
def ordr(key, k=2):
    d = Cv[key]; return [math.log(d[i - 1][k] / d[i][k]) / math.log(d[i - 1][0] / d[i][0]) for i in range(1, len(d))]
oH = ordr("dt_H_a0.6"); oR = ordr("dt_ramp_a0.6"); oRth = ordr("dt_ramp_a0.6", 1)
mt = M["MGT"]["snaps"]["0.4"]; ml = M["LS-type"]["snaps"]["0.4"]
dMGT = max(abs(a[k] - b[k]) for a, b in zip(mt, ml) for k in range(3))
pit = R["picard"]; pmean = sum(pit["iters"]) / len(pit["iters"])

H1("4. Results and discussion")
H2("4.1 Setup, characteristic speeds and the question of scale")
P("All computations use the dimensionless setup of the source study: $L=1$, $h=0.2$, $\\theta_0=1$, and the coefficients of Table 2. The relaxation times are $\\tau_0=0.1$ and $\\tau_1=0.05$, the laser pulse length is $\\tau_p=0.2$ and the reference temperature rise is $\\Delta T=10$ K. These values are illustrative: the source study does not give the relaxation times in a form that can be transferred, and a realistic tissue relaxation time of the order of seconds would be some $10^{11}$ to $10^{12}$ times larger than the dimensionless time unit of " + g(SC["tl"] * 1e12, 3) + " ps. "
  "The finite element production grid is $\\Delta x=\\Delta t=0.0025$, i.e. 400 elements and 240 steps to $t=0.6$; the time step of the source study, $0.01$, is used in the convergence study. The mapping of the dimensionless numbers to physical units is"); EQ(r"l=" + g(SC["l"]*1e9,3) + r"\ \mathrm{nm},\qquad \frac{l}{\nu}=" + g(SC["tl"]*1e12,3) + r"\ \mathrm{ps},\qquad \tau_0^{\mathrm{phys}}=0.1\,\frac{l}{\nu}=" + g(0.1*SC["tl"]*1e12,3) + r"\ \mathrm{ps}", "phys"); P("The characteristic speeds follow from the coefficients. In the tumour the thermal front of the integer-order model and the elastic front travel with")
EQ(r"c_{\mathrm{th},i}=\sqrt{\frac{\hat\kappa_i}{\hat C_i\tau_0}},\qquad c_{\mathrm{el},i}=\sqrt{\frac{a_{8i}}{\hat\rho_i}}", "speeds")
P("which gives $c_{\\mathrm{th},1}=" + f3(cth1) + "$ and $c_{\\mathrm{el},1}=1$ in the tumour and $c_{\\mathrm{th},2}=" + f3(cth2) + "$ and $c_{\\mathrm{el},2}=" + f3(ce2) + "$ in the normal tissue. The thermal front is thus faster than the elastic front in the tumour and slower in the normal tissue, and it reaches the interface at")
EQ(r"t_h=\frac{h}{c_{\mathrm{th},1}}=" + f3(th_arr), "th")
P("Because the thermoelastic coupling is weak ($a_{51}=" + sc(a1["a5"], 3) + "$), the temperature is almost independent of the displacement, and the elastic fields respond to the temperature through the thermal stress. The front amplitude decays as the lag term damps it, so that the integer-order front carries the amplitude")
EQ(r"\theta_{\mathrm{front}}(x)\approx\theta_0\exp\!\left(-\frac{x}{2\tau_0c_{\mathrm{th}}}\right)", "decay")
P("which is " + f3(math.exp(-0.2 / (2 * 0.1 * cth1))) + " at the interface and " + f3(math.exp(-1 / (2 * 0.1 * cth1))) + " at the far face when the difference between the layers is ignored. The remaining parameters of the model were fixed at the values of Table 2 for all runs unless stated.")
H2("4.2 Validation against the exact Laplace-domain solution")
P("Figure 2 compares the finite element temperature, displacement and stress histories with the numerically inverted Laplace-domain solution for $\\alpha=1$ and $\\alpha=0.6$, and Table 3 lists the relative $L_2$ errors over 19 points. The curves are sampled every $0.02$ time units, which draws the thermal front as a ramp. "
  "For $\\alpha=0.6$ the agreement is close at all times: at $t=0.4$ the errors in $\\theta$, $u$ and $\\Sigma$ are " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.4"]["th"]) + ", " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.4"]["u"]) + " and " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.4"]["sg"]) + " per cent. The error is larger at the earliest time shown ($t=0.1$: " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.1"]["th"]) + ", " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.1"]["u"]) + " and " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.1"]["sg"]) + " per cent) because the start-up error of the step boundary data has not yet been dispersed, and the stress error rises again at $t=0.6$ ("+ pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.6"]["sg"]) + " per cent) presumably when the reflected waves from the clamped face arrive. "
  "For $\\alpha=1$ the errors are larger: " + pct(V["H_a1_t0.4"]["th"]) + " per cent in $\\theta$ and " + pct(V["H_a1_t0.4"]["sg"]) + " per cent in $\\Sigma$ at $t=0.4$, and " + pct(V["H_a1_t0.1"]["th"]) + " and " + pct(V["H_a1_t0.1"]["sg"]) + " per cent at $t=0.1$. This is not a failure of the time stepping alone. The integer-order solution has a jump in temperature at the thermal front and a jump in stress at the elastic front, and a discrete solution with 400 elements and a second-order dissipative scheme smears a jump over several elements, which gives an $L_2$ error of order of the jump width divided by the sampled length. ")
FIG("Figure_2.png", "Finite element (solid and dotted) and Laplace-domain reference (dashed and dash-dotted) histories for $\\alpha=1$ and $\\alpha=0.6$: (a) temperature at $x=0.3$, (b) displacement at $x=0.1$, (c) stress at $x=0.1$. $\\Delta x=\\Delta t=0.0025$; the curves are sampled every 0.02 time units.")
TAB(*T4())
P("Table 3 shows this directly. When the points within 0.08 of the estimated thermal and elastic fronts are excluded, the errors for $\\alpha=1$ fall to " + pct(EX["masked_t0.2"]["th"]) + " per cent in $\\theta$, " + pct(EX["masked_t0.2"]["u"]) + " per cent in $u$ and " + pct(EX["masked_t0.2"]["sg"]) + " per cent in $\\Sigma$ at $t=0.2$, and to " + pct(EX["masked_t0.4"]["th"]) + ", " + pct(EX["masked_t0.4"]["u"]) + " and " + pct(EX["masked_t0.4"]["sg"]) + " per cent at $t=0.4$. The masked errors at $t=0.1$ are larger for the displacement (" + pct(EX["masked_t0.1"]["u"]) + " per cent). "
  "The remaining error of the integer-order case is not fully explained. Part of it is the Laplace reference itself, whose own uncertainty near the fronts at $t=0.5$ was quantified in Section 3.1, and part is the first-order start-up error described in Section 4.3. The integer-order stress, which contains a jump at the elastic front, is the least accurate quantity, and the stress peaks reported below for $\\alpha=1$ should be read as mesh-dependent magnitudes.")
P("The laser-driven linear problem ($\\theta_0=0$, $a_7=2$, $\\alpha=0.7$) has errors of " + pct(V["L_a0.7_t0.2"]["th"]) + ", " + pct(V["L_a0.7_t0.2"]["u"]) + " and " + pct(V["L_a0.7_t0.2"]["sg"]) + " per cent at $t=0.2$, and " + pct(V["L_a0.7_t0.4"]["th"]) + ", " + pct(V["L_a0.7_t0.4"]["u"]) + " and " + pct(V["L_a0.7_t0.4"]["sg"]) + " per cent at $t=0.4$, which validates the load vector and the exponential particular solution. "
  "The error in $\\theta$ at $t=0.6$ is larger (" + pct(V["L_a0.7_t0.6"]["th"]) + " per cent) because the temperature there is small after the pulse has ended and the relative measure magnifies absolute differences; the pulse end at $t=0.2$ is a discontinuity of the source that the time stepping resolves with first-order accuracy. The Pennes limit ($\\tau_0=\\tau_1=0$) and the case $\\tau_1=0$ with $\\alpha=0.8$ give temperature errors of " + pct(V["pennes"]["th"]) + " and " + pct(V["tau1_0"]["th"]) + " per cent, which shows that the finite element code reproduces the parabolic limit and the LS-type limit of the exact solution. The stress errors for these two cases are about 11 to 12 per cent, because without the strain-rate lag the stress contains the unregularised elastic jump.")
H2("4.3 Convergence in time and space")
P("Table 4 and Figure 3 give the errors for decreasing $\\Delta t$ and $\\Delta x$. For the fractional case $\\alpha=0.6$ with the step (Heaviside) boundary data the error in $u$ halves with every halving of $\\Delta t$, with observed orders " + ", ".join("%.2f" % o for o in oH) + ", i.e. first order, whereas the same scheme with a smooth ramp $\\theta_0(1-e^{-t/0.05})$ shows observed orders " + ", ".join("%.2f" % o for o in oR) + " for $u$ and " + ", ".join("%.2f" % o for o in oRth) + " for $\\theta$, i.e. second order. ")
TAB(*T3())
P("The loss of order for the step data is a start-up effect of the convolution quadrature: the surface value jumps in one step, the solution near the surface is presumably singular in time at $t=0$, and the quadrature, which assumes smooth data, makes an error proportional to $\\Delta t$ there. The effect of centring the step on $t=0$ (Eq. (@bcnode@)) was tested and changes the error by less than 1 per cent, so the first-order behaviour is a property of the singular start and not of the sampling of the step. "
  "For the integer-order case the step data again give about first order in $u$ (" + ", ".join("%.2f" % o for o in ordr("dt_H_a1")) + "), while the ramp data give an order between 1 and 1.3 only in $u$, because the ramp still produces a front across which the derivative jumps. The error model that summarises these observations is")
EQ(r"E\approx C_t\,\Delta t^{\,p_t}+C_x\,\Delta x^{2},\qquad p_t=\begin{cases}\end{cases}".replace(r"p_t=\begin{cases}\end{cases}", r"p_t\approx1\ (\text{step data}),\quad p_t\approx2\ (\text{smooth data})"), "errmod")
P("The mesh study at $\\Delta t=0.0025$ shows second-order convergence in space until the time-stepping error is reached. For the smooth data the error in $\\theta$ falls from " + pct(Cv["dx_ramp_a0.6"][0][1]) + " per cent at $\\Delta x=0.04$ to " + pct(Cv["dx_ramp_a0.6"][1][1]) + " per cent at $0.02$ and " + pct(Cv["dx_ramp_a0.6"][2][1]) + " per cent at $0.01$, the ratios being 4.1 and 4.3, and it then stays at the level of the time error. "
  "The error in $\\Sigma$ drops more erratically because the stress is evaluated on element mid-points, whose positions change with the mesh. A mesh of 200 elements is already sufficient for the temperature, and the production mesh of 400 elements is conservative. The conclusion for the later studies is that the time step $\\Delta t=0.0025$ gives an error in $u$ of about " + pct(Cv["dt_H_a0.6"][3][2]) + " per cent for $\\alpha=0.6$ at $t=0.4$, and the source value $\\Delta t=0.01$ gives " + pct(Cv["dt_H_a0.6"][1][2]) + " per cent.")
FIG("Figure_3.png", "Convergence and iteration behaviour: (a) displacement error against time step for step (Heaviside) and smooth-ramp boundary data, (b) temperature and displacement errors against mesh size at $\\Delta t=0.0025$, (c) Picard residual at the last time step for $\\Pi=10$ with the perfusion law and $\\chi_1=0.2$.")
H2("4.4 Limit checks and the nonlinear terms")
P("Four limits were checked. First, the fractional model approaches the integer-order model as $\\alpha\\to1$: the largest nodal difference in $\\theta$ at $t=0.4$ between $\\alpha=0.999$ and $\\alpha=1$ is " + sct(LM["alpha_0.999_vs_1"], 2) + ", and between $\\alpha=0.9$ and $\\alpha=1$ it is " + sct(LM["alpha_0.9_vs_1"], 2) + ". The ratio of the two numbers corresponds to a sensitivity")
EQ(r"\max_x\left|\theta_{\alpha}-\theta_{1}\right|\approx s_\alpha\,(1-\alpha),\qquad s_\alpha\approx2.8\ (\alpha=0.999),\quad 0.64\ (\alpha=0.9)", "salpha")
P("which is large near $\\alpha=1$ because the sharp front of the integer-order model is replaced by a smooth precursor as soon as $\\alpha<1$; the limit is continuous in an integral sense but not uniformly at the front. "
  "Second, the limit of zero perfusion is exact: with the literal perfusion number $a_6=" + sc(LM["a6_literal"], 2) + "$ the solution is identical to the solution with $a_6=0$ to the last digit of double precision, which confirms that the literal setup is a perfusion-free problem. "
  "Third, the nonlinear code path with $c_w$ reduced to $10^{-12}$ reproduces the linear solution exactly (maximum difference zero) " + "after " + str(LM["cw0_iters"]) + " iterations, so that $c_w=0$ recovers the linear case exactly. Fourth, the tumour-edge stress with $\\tau_1=0$ is smaller by a factor " + f2(edge_peak("base")[0] / edge_peak("tau1_0")[0]) + " than with $\\tau_1=0.05$, which is discussed in Section 4.7.")
P("The nonlinear terms are verified by the steady-state check of Eq. (@steady@). For the perfusion law with $\\Pi=20$ the finite element solution at $t=8$ (obtained with $\\Delta x=0.005$ and $\\Delta t=0.05$) differs from the independent finite volume solution by " + sct(STEADY[0][1], 2) + " in the maximum norm, and for the case with a variable tumour conductivity $\\chi_1=0.2$ in addition by " + sct(STEADY[1][1], 2) + ". These differences are consistent with the spatial error of the coarse finite element mesh. "
  "The Picard iteration converged at every step. For the case of Figure 3(c) the first steps need 8 iterations, the last steps 3, with a mean of " + f2(pmean) + " per step, and at the last step the residuals are " + ", ".join("%.1e" % v for v in pit["resid"]) + ", so that each iteration reduces the residual by a factor of about $10^{3}$ (superlinear convergence). With only the perfusion law and moderate $\\Pi$ the maximum number of iterations is 4 to 5, and with $\\chi_1=0.3$ it is " + str(S["chi"]["iters_max"]) + ". "
  "In summary form, over all steps of the case of Figure 3(c),")
EQ(r"\bar N_{\mathrm{it}}=" + f2(pmean) + r",\qquad \max N_{\mathrm{it}}=" + str(max(pit["iters"])) + r",\qquad \varrho_k\approx" + "%.0e" % (pit["resid"][-1] / pit["resid"][-2]), "pstat")
P("The most demanding part of the iteration is the first step, where the step in the boundary data creates large temperature gradients. No failure of convergence was observed.")
H2("4.5 Comparison of the Pennes, MGT, MGL and fractional MGL models")
P("The linear models are compared with the Laplace-domain solution. Figure 4 gives temperature profiles at $t=0.1$ and $t=0.4$ and the stress profile at $t=0.1$ for the Pennes model ($\\tau_0=\\tau_1=0$), the MGT model of [13] with $\\tau_q=0.1$, the LS-type model ($\\tau_1=0$), the MGL model, and the fractional MGL model with $\\alpha=0.75$ and $0.5$. "
  "The result that matters most is that the MGT and LS-type curves coincide: the largest difference of any field over the profile at $t=0.4$ is " + sct(dMGT, 1) + ". The reason is the MGT parameter $a_3=k^{*}l/(k_{10}\\nu)$. With the source values this parameter is " + sct(a1["a3"], 2) + ", and the additional term $a_3/s$ in $\\kappa_e$ has no effect:")
EQ(r"\frac{a_{31}}{\hat\kappa_1}=" + sc(a1["a3"], 2) + r"\ \ll\ 1\quad\Rightarrow\quad \kappa_e\approx\hat\kappa\ \ (\text{MGT}\approx\text{LS-type})", "mgtls")
P("This agrees with the observation of the source study that the MGT and LS responses are close. The closeness is a property of the data, since $a_3$ is proportional to $k^{*}l/(k\\nu)$ and the ratio $k^{*}/(k\\nu)$ is small for the values of Table 1: even for a length unit $l=1$ mm the parameter would be about $1.7\\times10^{-4}$, and the MGT correction would remain small. The nanometre length unit makes it smaller still. ")
FIG("Figure_4.png", "Linear model comparison from the Laplace-domain solution ($h=0.2$, $\\tau_0=\\tau_q=0.1$, $\\tau_1=0.05$): temperature at (a) $t=0.1$ and (b) $t=0.4$, and (c) stress at $t=0.1$, for the Pennes, MGT, LS-type, MGL ($\\alpha=1$) and fractional MGL ($\\alpha=0.75$, $0.5$) models.")
P("The Pennes temperature at $t=0.1$ is " + f3(mrow("Pennes", 0.1, 0.3)[0]) + " at $x=0.3$ against " + f3(mrow("MGL", 0.1, 0.3)[0]) + " for the hyperbolic models, and at $x=0.5$ it is " + f3(mrow("Pennes", 0.1, 0.5)[0]) + " against zero. This is the infinite-speed property of the parabolic model, which carries a heat signal far ahead of the thermal wave. At $t=0.4$ the temperature at $x=0.5$ is " + f3(mrow("Pennes", 0.4, 0.5)[0]) + " (Pennes), " + f3(mrow("MGL", 0.4, 0.5)[0]) + " (MGL), " + f3(mrow("fMGL0.75", 0.4, 0.5)[0]) + " ($\\alpha=0.75$) and " + f3(mrow("fMGL0.5", 0.4, 0.5)[0]) + " ($\\alpha=0.5$). "
  "The hyperbolic models are hotter than the Pennes model at this time presumably because the wave has reflected from the isothermal far face and the lag retains heat, and the fractional models lie between the Pennes and the integer-order curves, as expected from an operator that interpolates between diffusion and a wave. "
  "The MGL and LS-type temperatures are the same to plotting accuracy, as they should be since $\\tau_1$ enters only the elastic equation and the weak coupling lets the displacement feed back on the temperature only at order $a_5$. The strain-rate lag changes the stress. At $t=0.1$ and $x=0.1$ the stress is " + f3(mrow("LS-type", 0.1, 0.1)[2]) + " without the lag, " + f3(mrow("MGL", 0.1, 0.1)[2]) + " with $\\tau_1=0.05$, " + f3(mrow("fMGL0.75", 0.1, 0.1)[2]) + " for $\\alpha=0.75$ and " + f3(mrow("fMGL0.5", 0.1, 0.1)[2]) + " for $\\alpha=0.5$, with Pennes at " + f3(mrow("Pennes", 0.1, 0.1)[2]) + ". ")
P("A word on the size of the thermoelastic feedback is in order. The coupling coefficient $a_{51}=" + sc(a1["a5"], 2) + "$ is the ratio of the heat absorbed by the volumetric deformation to the heat stored thermally, and with its value the effect of the displacement on the temperature is below the plotting accuracy in all figures: switching the coupling off changes the temperature by a relative amount of the order of $a_5$ times the dilatation, which is about $10^{-4}$ here. The temperature field is therefore a thermal problem on its own, and the displacement and the stress are driven by it. This property simplifies the interpretation of the fractional and perfusion effects, since they act on the stress only through the temperature, but it also shows that the coupled character of the MGL theory is not tested by the dimensionless setup inherited from the source study. The ratio $a_{51}/a_{41}=T_0\\beta_1^{2}/(\\rho_1c_{v1}(\\lambda_1+2\\mu_1))$ does not depend on the length unit, so that the weakness of the feedback is a property of the material data of Table 1.")
H2("4.6 Effect of the fractional order")
P("Figure 5 shows the finite element temperature and stress for $\\alpha$ between 1 and 0.3. The effect of the order is first of all on the shape of the front. For $\\alpha<1$ the large-$s$ behaviour of the wave number is")
EQ(r"q(s)\sim\sqrt{\frac{\hat C}{\hat\kappa}\,\tau_0^{\alpha}}\;s^{(1+\alpha)/2},\qquad \mathrm{Re}\,q(s)\ \text{grows more slowly than}\ s\ \text{when}\ \alpha<1", "qlarge")
P("so that the inverse transform has no finite front, and heat reaches distant points earlier than it does in the integer-order model. The computed leading edge defined in Eq. (@diag@) confirms this. At $t=0.1$ it is at $x_f=" + f2(S["base"]["front"]["0.1"]) + "$ for $\\alpha=1$, " + f2(S["alpha_0.9"]["front"]["0.1"]) + " for $0.9$, " + f2(S["alpha_0.75"]["front"]["0.1"]) + " for $0.75$, " + f2(S["alpha_0.45"]["front"]["0.1"]) + " for $0.45$ and " + f2(S["alpha_0.3"]["front"]["0.1"]) + " for $0.3$. "
  "The temperature at $x=0.5$ and $t=0.1$ is " + f4(S["base"]["snaps"]["0.1"][50][0]) + ", " + f4(S["alpha_0.75"]["snaps"]["0.1"][50][0]) + " and " + f4(S["alpha_0.45"]["snaps"]["0.1"][50][0]) + " for $\\alpha=1$, $0.75$ and $0.45$. The precursor is small but not zero, and it is a model prediction of non-local, memory-type conduction. The integer-order threshold position at $t=0.05$ is $" + f2(S["base"]["front"]["0.05"]) + "$ whereas the wave arithmetic gives $" + f3(cth1 * 0.05) + "$; the difference is the numerical smearing of the jump and the threshold of $10^{-3}$.")
P("The second effect is that decreasing $\\alpha$ lowers the temperature behind the early part of the disturbance. The tumour-edge temperature at $t=0.2$ is " + f3(th_at("base", 2, 0.2)) + ", " + f3(th_at("alpha_0.75", 2, 0.2)) + ", " + f3(th_at("alpha_0.45", 2, 0.2)) + " for $\\alpha=1$, $0.75$, $0.45$, and at $t=0.6$ it is " + f3(th_at("base", 2, 0.6)) + ", " + f3(th_at("alpha_0.75", 2, 0.6)) + " and " + f3(th_at("alpha_0.45", 2, 0.6)) + ". The ordering reverses with time: the fractional models heat the edge more slowly at first because the slower relaxation spreads the heat, and they are slightly warmer later. "
  "The third effect is on the stress. The peak tumour-edge stress magnitude of Eq. (@diag@) is " + f2(edge_peak("base")[0]) + ", " + f2(edge_peak("alpha_0.9")[0]) + ", " + f2(edge_peak("alpha_0.75")[0]) + ", " + f2(edge_peak("alpha_0.6")[0]) + ", " + f2(edge_peak("alpha_0.45")[0]) + " and " + f2(edge_peak("alpha_0.3")[0]) + " for $\\alpha=1,\\dots,0.3$, a monotone reduction by a factor " + f2(edge_peak("base")[0] / edge_peak("alpha_0.3")[0]) + " over the range, because the smooth precursor replaces the steep thermal front that drives the stress. "
  "The integer-order value is the most mesh-dependent of the group, as noted in Section 4.2. The relation between the arrival time of the front at a distance $x$ and the order is not a simple power in the present parameters, and no scaling law is claimed.")
FIG("Figure_5.png", "Effect of the fractional order $\\alpha$ (finite element, $h=0.2$, $\\tau_0=0.1$, $\\tau_1=0.05$): (a) temperature at $t=0.2$, (b) temperature at the tumour edge $x=h$ as a function of time, (c) stress at $t=0.2$.")
H2("4.7 Effect of the strain-rate lag")
P("The strain-rate lag $\\tau_1$ changes the stress and the displacement but leaves the temperature unchanged to plotting accuracy (the temperature at $x=0.5$ and $t=0.4$ is " + f4(th_at("base", 4, 0.4)) + " for $\\tau_1=0.05$ and " + f4(th_at("tau1_0", 4, 0.4)) + " for $\\tau_1=0$), as expected for a weakly coupled system. Figure 7(a) shows the stress history at $x=0.1$. "
  "The tumour-edge peak stress is " + f2(edge_peak("tau1_0")[0]) + ", " + f2(edge_peak("tau1_0.025")[0]) + ", " + f2(edge_peak("base")[0]) + ", " + f2(edge_peak("tau1_0.1")[0]) + " and " + f2(edge_peak("tau1_0.2")[0]) + " for $\\tau_1=0,0.025,0.05,0.1,0.2$. The increase of the stress with $\\tau_1$ is saturating, and it is a viscous contribution: the lag adds a term proportional to the strain rate, which is largest when the heating front passes. "
  "For the displacement at the tumour edge at $t=0.6$ the values are " + f3(u_at("tau1_0", 2, 0.6)) + ", " + f3(u_at("base", 2, 0.6)) + " and " + f3(u_at("tau1_0.2", 2, 0.6)) + " for $\\tau_1=0$, $0.05$ and $0.2$. In terms of the ratio to the lag-free value")
EQ(r"\frac{\Sigma_{\max}(\tau_1)}{\Sigma_{\max}(0)}=" + f2(edge_peak("tau1_0.025")[0] / edge_peak("tau1_0")[0]) + r"\ (0.025),\quad " + f2(edge_peak("base")[0] / edge_peak("tau1_0")[0]) + r"\ (0.05),\quad " + f2(edge_peak("tau1_0.2")[0] / edge_peak("tau1_0")[0]) + r"\ (0.2)", "tau1ratio")
P("A higher peak with a larger lag is the opposite of the smoothing that is usually associated with a regularising term. The explanation is that the peak in question is an early-time magnitude reached near the surface heating shock, where the strain-rate term is large; the later stress, for example at $x=0.1$ and $t=0.3$, is " + f3(sig_at("tau1_0", 1, 0.3)) + " without the lag and " + f3(sig_at("base", 1, 0.3)) + " with $\\tau_1=0.05$, and it is smaller in magnitude with the lag. The MGL term removes the displacement discontinuity but does not reduce the stress peak. The thermal relaxation time $\\tau_0$ acts in the opposite direction: the peak edge stress is " + f2(edge_peak("tau0_0.05")[0]) + " for $\\tau_0=0.05$, " + f2(edge_peak("base")[0]) + " for $0.1$ and " + f2(edge_peak("tau0_0.2")[0]) + " for $0.2$ at $\\alpha=1$.")
H2("4.8 Temperature-dependent perfusion and conductivity")
P("The perfusion studies use the law of Eq. (@omega@), shown in Figure 6(a), with the perfusion number $\\Pi$ of the tumour as a free parameter. Since the perfusion length of Eq. (@ellw@) is " + g(math.sqrt(0.778 / (0.009 * 1060 * 3770)) * 1e3, 3) + " mm and the dimensionless slab has the length $1$, the perfusion length measured in the units of the slab is")
EQ(r"\ell_p=\sqrt{\frac{\hat\kappa_1}{a_{61}}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\Pi}}\ \ \Rightarrow\ \ \ell_p=" + f2(1 / math.sqrt(2)) + r"\ (\Pi=2),\ \ " + f2(1 / math.sqrt(10)) + r"\ (\Pi=10),\ \ " + f2(1 / math.sqrt(20)) + r"\ (\Pi=20)", "ellp")
P("For the values $\\Pi=2$ to $20$ the perfusion length is thus between $0.7$ and $0.22$, comparable to the size of the slab, and the perfusion has a visible effect on the steady temperature profile. In the literal setup, $\\Pi=" + sc(a1["a6"], 2) + "$, the effect is nil, as shown in Section 4.4. "
  "With a constant perfusion the temperature at the tumour edge at $t=0.6$ falls from " + f3(snapth("Pi_0", 0.6, 0.2)) + " at $\\Pi=0$ to " + f3(snapth("Pi_10_c0", 0.6, 0.2)) + " at $\\Pi=10$ and " + f3(snapth("Pi_20_c0", 0.6, 0.2)) + " at $\\Pi=20$. With the temperature-dependent law the same numbers are " + f3(snapth("Pi_2", 0.6, 0.2)) + " at $\\Pi=2$, " + f3(snapth("Pi_10", 0.6, 0.2)) + " at $\\Pi=10$ and " + f3(snapth("Pi_20", 0.6, 0.2)) + " at $\\Pi=20$. The reduction relative to the perfusion-free solution is much smaller with the law, and it can be expressed by")
EQ(r"\Delta_w=\frac{\theta(h,0.6;\Pi=0)-\theta(h,0.6;\Pi)}{\theta(h,0.6;\Pi=0)}:\quad \Delta_w^{\mathrm{const}}=" + f3(1 - snapth("Pi_10_c0", 0.6, 0.2) / snapth("Pi_0", 0.6, 0.2)) + r",\ \ \Delta_w^{\omega}=" + f3(1 - snapth("Pi_10", 0.6, 0.2) / snapth("Pi_0", 0.6, 0.2)) + r"\ \ (\Pi=10)", "deltaw")
P("The explanation is the shutdown part of the law. The tumour edge is heated above the critical value $\\theta_{c1}=0.6$ (about 316 K), at which the tumour perfusion multiplier falls through one half and then to nearly zero, so that the heat sink is removed exactly where the temperature is highest. The model therefore predicts that a tumour whose perfusion collapses during heating retains the heat much better than the constant-perfusion Pennes model suggests, and this feature is the main effect of the nonlinearity. "
  "It follows from the assumed law, whose parameters are illustrative and which has no experimental support in this paper. With the vasodilation slope alone (a law without shutdown) the effect would have the opposite sign. Figure 6(b) shows the profiles at $t=0.6$ for several $\\Pi$ and Figure 6(c) the comparison with constant perfusion at $\\Pi=10$. "
  "A fourfold stronger vasodilation slope in both layers (Table 5, $c_w$ rows) lowers the edge temperature at $t=0.3$ from " + f3(th_at("Pi_10", 2, 0.3)) + " to " + f3(th_at("cw_4", 2, 0.3)) + ", so that the sensitivity to $c_w$ is real but modest compared with the effect of the shutdown. The deeper normal tissue is less affected: at $x=0.5$, $t=0.6$ the temperature is " + f3(snapth("Pi_0", 0.6, 0.5)) + " ($\\Pi=0$), " + f3(snapth("Pi_10", 0.6, 0.5)) + " (law) and " + f3(snapth("Pi_10_c0", 0.6, 0.5)) + " (constant), where the normal-tissue perfusion is smaller by the factor $r_w=" + f3(WR) + "$.")
FIG("Figure_6.png", "Temperature-dependent perfusion: (a) the multiplier $\\omega(\\theta)$ for the tumour and the normal tissue, (b) temperature profiles at $t=0.6$ for several perfusion numbers $\\Pi$ with the nonlinear law, (c) comparison of constant and temperature-dependent perfusion at $\\Pi=10$. $\\alpha=0.75$, $h=0.2$.")
P("The variable tumour conductivity with $\\chi_1=0.3$ raises the tumour-edge temperature at $t=0.3$ from " + f3(th_at("alpha_0.75", 2, 0.3)) + " to " + f3(th_at("chi", 2, 0.3)) + " and the peak edge stress from " + f2(edge_peak("alpha_0.75")[0]) + " to " + f2(edge_peak("chi")[0]) + ", and it needs up to " + str(S["chi"]["iters_max"]) + " Picard iterations. This agrees in direction with the tumour-only effect of the variable conductivity reported in [13]. Adding the perfusion law on top of $\\chi_1=0.3$ lowers the edge temperature to " + f3(th_at("chi_cw", 2, 0.3)) + ". The numerical entries are in Table 5.")
TAB(*T5())
H2("4.9 Interface position and the fields beyond the interface")
P("The source study reports that the fields vanish beyond the interface for the times considered. In the present solution this holds only for very short times. The thermal front of the integer-order model reaches the interface at $t_h=" + f3(th_arr) + "$ (Eq. (@th@)), and at $t=0.05$ the temperature at $x=0.3$ is " + f4(S["base"]["snaps"]["0.05"][30][0]) + " for $\\alpha=1$, while for $\\alpha=0.75$ it is " + f4(S["alpha_0.75"]["snaps"]["0.05"][30][0]) + " and for $\\alpha=0.45$ it is " + f4(S["alpha_0.45"]["snaps"]["0.05"][30][0]) + ". After the arrival time the temperature is large beyond the interface: at $x=0.3$ and $t=0.1$ it is " + f3(S["base"]["snaps"]["0.1"][30][0]) + ", at $x=0.5$ and $t=0.2$ it is " + f3(S["base"]["snaps"]["0.2"][50][0]) + " and at $t=0.4$ it is " + f3(S["base"]["snaps"]["0.4"][50][0]) + " for $\\alpha=1$. "
  "The statement of the source study is therefore not reproduced for $\\tau_0=0.1$. It could hold for the source parameters if their relaxation time is much smaller than the one assumed here, or if the times shown are shorter than $h/c_{\\mathrm{th}}$, but this cannot be checked because the relaxation times of the source calculations are not transferable. The fraction of the slab that carries a temperature above the threshold is measured by the leading edge $x_f$ of Eq. (@diag@):")
EQ(r"x_f(0.05)=" + f2(S["base"]["front"]["0.05"]) + r",\quad x_f(0.1)=" + f2(S["base"]["front"]["0.1"]) + r",\quad x_f(0.2)=" + f2(S["base"]["front"]["0.2"]) + r"\quad(\alpha=1)", "xf")
P("so that the whole slab is heated at $t=0.4$. For the elastic fields the same holds: the displacement at $x=0.5$ is " + f3(u_at("base", 4, 0.1)) + " at $t=0.1$ and " + f3(u_at("base", 4, 0.4)) + " at $t=0.4$. The result applies to the nanometre-scale slab of the dimensionless setup, where the diffusion time across the slab is of the order of one time unit and the time window to $0.6$ spans a large part of it. "
  "Figure 7(b) shows the temperature at $t=0.2$ for four interface positions with $\\alpha=0.75$. A larger tumour layer moves the interface away from the heated surface: the tumour-edge temperature at $t=0.2$ is " + f3(snapth("h_0.1", 0.2, 0.1)) + ", " + f3(snapth("h_0.2", 0.2, 0.2)) + ", " + f3(snapth("h_0.3", 0.2, 0.3)) + " and " + f3(snapth("h_0.4", 0.2, 0.4)) + " for $h=0.1,\\dots,0.4$, which decreases with $h$ because the edge is farther from the heated surface. The tumour-edge stress peak decreases from " + f2(edge_peak("h_0.1")[0]) + " to " + f2(edge_peak("h_0.4")[0]) + " over the same range. ")
EQ(r"\Sigma_{\max}(h):\quad " + f2(edge_peak("h_0.1")[0]) + r",\ " + f2(edge_peak("h_0.2")[0]) + r",\ " + f2(edge_peak("h_0.3")[0]) + r",\ " + f2(edge_peak("h_0.4")[0]) + r"\quad(h=0.1,0.2,0.3,0.4)", "hstress")
P("The reflection of the thermal wave at the isothermal far face, which occurs at about $t=0.32$ for the integer-order model, raises the temperature in the normal tissue again. For late times the temperature profile approaches the linear steady profile of a slab held at one and zero, and this is the reason for the weak dependence of the late-time temperature on the model at $t=0.6$ visible in Figures 4 and 5.")
FIG("Figure_7.png", "(a) Stress history at $x=0.1$ for several strain-rate lags $\\tau_1$ ($\\alpha=1$), (b) temperature profile at $t=0.2$ for interface positions $h=0.1$ to $0.4$ ($\\alpha=0.75$), (c) temperature far beyond the interface at $x=0.5$ for $\\alpha=1$, $0.75$ and $0.45$.")
P("Panel (c) shows the temperature at $x=0.5$ as a function of time. For $\\alpha=1$ it stays at zero until the front arrives at about $t=0.17$, for $\\alpha=0.45$ it starts to rise immediately, and at $t=0.6$ all three curves are between 0.52 and 0.56, i.e. the long-time state is nearly independent of $\\alpha$, which is expected because the steady state does not contain the lag.")
H2("4.10 Laser-driven response")
P("A laser-only case with $\\theta_0=0$, $a_7=2$ and a pulse of length $\\tau_p=0.2$ completes the study. Since the literal $a_7$ is of order $10^{-12}$, the value 2 is an illustrative rescaling in the same sense as $\\Pi$. Because the optical depth is $\\delta^{*}=" + sc(LS["deltas"], 2) + "$, the source is uniform in the slab and the steady temperature under a constant uniform source with both faces held at zero is bounded by")
EQ(r"\theta_{\max}^{\mathrm{ss}}=\frac{a_7\,g(0)}{8\,\hat\kappa}\approx\frac{2\times" + f3(LS["B1"] - LS["B2"]) + r"}{8}=" + f3(2 * (LS["B1"] - LS["B2"]) / 8), "laserss")
P("The rescaling is large. The intensity that would give $a_7=2$ at the nanometre length unit follows from the definition of $a_7$,")
EQ(r"\frac{I_0^{\mathrm{eff}}}{I_0}=\frac{2}{a_7^{\mathrm{lit}}}=" + sc(2 / A7, 2), "i0eff")
P("so that the laser-only case is a statement about the dimensionless equations and not about a realistic irradiance. The computed temperature at $x=0.5$ for $\\alpha=0.75$ reaches " + f3(R["laser"]["snaps"]["0.2"][50][0]) + " at the end of the pulse ($t=0.2$), which is below the steady bound, as it must be since the pulse is shorter than the diffusion time, and it decays to " + f3(R["laser"]["snaps"]["0.4"][50][0]) + " at $t=0.4$. The tumour-edge value is " + f3(R["laser"]["snaps"]["0.2"][20][0]) + " at $t=0.2$. With the perfusion law active ($\\Pi=10$) the maximum value at $x=0.5$ falls to " + f3(R["laser_perf"]["snaps"]["0.2"][50][0]) + ". "
  "The perfusion effect is small because the heating is short and the temperature stays below the critical value of the tumour law. The temperature at $x=0.5$ becomes slightly negative at later times ($" + f3(R["laser"]["snaps"]["0.6"][50][0]) + "$ at $t=0.6$), an undershoot that is a feature of the Dirichlet faces and of the relaxation, and it is not a numerical instability since it is small and appears in the Laplace-domain solution as well (laser validation of Table 3).")
H2("4.11 Limitations")
P("The results are subject to the following limits, all of which affect interpretation. The wavelet collocation method requested for the extension was not implemented, and the finite element scheme has first-order accuracy for the step boundary data. The integer-order case suffers from front smearing and from a degraded Laplace reference near fronts. "
  "The relaxation times, the vasodilation and shutdown parameters, the conductivity slope and the rescaled perfusion and laser numbers are illustrative, and a different choice would change the numbers, although not the structure of the findings. The nanometre-scale length unit and the picosecond time unit mean that the dimensionless slab is not a clinical geometry, and the literal perfusion and laser terms vanish in it. "
  "Nothing in this paper is a clinical prediction, and the stress magnitudes in particular are dimensionless model values without a failure criterion.")
